    "q-168009": {
      "dom_id": "question-168009",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The function $g$ is defined as $g(x)={\\int }_{-5}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to -5}\\,\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}+12x+35}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that, to calculate $g(-5),$ the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\ng(-5)={\\int }_{-5}^{-5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0\n$$\n\nSo, plugging in $x=-5$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -5}\\,\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}+12x+35} & ={lim}_{x\\to -5}\\,\\frac{{g}^{′}(x)}{[{x}^{2}+12x+35]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -5}\\,\\frac{{g}^{′}(x)}{2x+12} \\\\  & =\\frac{{g}^{′}(-5)}{2\\cdot (-5)+12} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(-5)}{-10+12} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
